            <ArticleTitle>Postsurgical segmental mesenteric ischemic thrombosis in a horse.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>83-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 16-year-old, Lusitanian stallion was admitted to the Veterinary Teaching Hospital with a 12-hour history of signs of abdominal pain. Exploratory celiotomy was performed due to an inguinal hernia, and a second celiotomy was performed in response to the abdominal pain. The horse was euthanized and mesenteric venous thrombosis was diagnosed and considered likely due to peritonitis and systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
